    "body": "* Fix next() recording immediately after ITERN despecialization.\n* Handle OOM error during trace stitching.\n* FFI: Mark cts->L in atomic phase.\n* Compile unpack().\n",
    "closed_at": "2026-08-19T11:40:03Z",
    "comment_data": [],
    "comment_regular_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "this may be causing the flaky lsp test failures. AI analysis:\r\n\r\n> upstream 52cdb12eb \"Compile unpack().\" — LuaJIT now JIT-compiles unpack(), and the compiled path intermittently returns fewer values than the table holds.\r\n> \r\n> Evidence. Instrumenting vim.range.mark caught the arguments and the caller:\r\n> \r\n> ```\r\n> XXDBG args={ 1, 2, 2, 2 }\r\n> TB= util.lua:1890 in 'make_given_range_params'\r\n>     buf.lua:675 in 'fn' → shared:286 in 'tbl_map' → buf.lua:682 'set_range' → buf.lua:709 'format'\r\n> ```\r\n> \r\n> Five parameters, four values: buf=1, start_lnum=2, start_col=2, end_lnum=2, end_col=nil. The source line is\r\n> \r\n>     local end_row, end_col = unpack(end_pos or api.nvim_buf_get_mark(bufnr, '>'))\r\n> \r\n> with end_pos = {2, 2} — a literal two-element table in the test. unpack returned one value. tbl_map is the hot loop that gets the trace compiled, which is why all three failures are vim.lsp.buf.format range paths and why they're\r\n> intermittent.\r\n> \r\n> ",
            "created_at": "2026-08-19T13:28:44Z",
